IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mc97-nouns`

-- MAGNA CARTA (1297), LAYER 1: THE GENERAL SAVING, three unnumbered paragraphs.
-- Source: aep-Edw1cc1929-25-9-extract.md, the heading "General Saving. Observance of these Liberties. Subsidy, in respect of
-- this Charter and Charter of the Forest." (line 37) and the paragraphs the extract calls wrapper1 (line 41), wrapper2 (line 44)
-- and wrapper3 (line 47).
-- The extract's author reads these three paragraphs as the unrepealed part of chapter 37. The XML does not say so in so many
-- words, and NOTHING in the rules below depends on it (fork F-SAVING-C37).
-- Nothing here says that any of it is, or ever was, in force.
--
-- "Reserving" and "have given" and "have granted" are statements of what was reserved, given and granted; "we shall observe",
-- "shall observe" and "shall be ... observed" are duties; "it shall be had of no force nor effect" is a nullity.
-- The witness list, the "( )" gaps and the attestation create no rule: they are carried in NOTES.md as inert (reason 1).
-- The words "Fifteenth Part" are the text's own; the text names no unit for Moveables, so the amount is a ratio, not money.

§ `General Saving. Observance of these Liberties. Subsidy, in respect of this Charter and Charter of the Forest.`

§§ `First paragraph: the reservation`

-- gloss (not the text's): the reservation names eight classes and then "all Persons, as well Spiritual as Temporal". Every
-- capacity this encoding knows is a Person, so every capacity is reached; the rule says by which words.
-- src:41 | Reserving to all Archbishops, Bishops, Abbots, Priors, Templars, Hospitallers, Earls, Barons, and all Persons, as well Spiritual as Temporal,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, the persons reserved to (src:41)
GIVEN capacity IS A Capacity
GIVETH A `How a capacity is reached`
`first paragraph: how the reservation reaches` capacity MEANS
    IF      capacity EQUALS `an Archbishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Bishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Abbot`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Prior`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Templar`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Hospitaller`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Earl`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Baron`
    THEN `named in the list`
    ELSE `reached as one of all Persons`

-- gloss (not the text's): "free" is read as qualifying both Liberties and Customs, as printed ("their free Liberties and free
-- Customs"). The extract's variant table has "Liberties" without "free" here (fork F-SAVING-VAR1).
-- src:41 | all their free Liberties and free Customs, which they have had in time passed.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, what is reserved (src:41)
GIVEN `the Liberty or Custom is free` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they have had it in time passed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Coverage
`first paragraph: the reservation reserves` `the Liberty or Custom is free` `they have had it in time passed` MEANS
    IF `the Liberty or Custom is free` AND `they have had it in time passed`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, silent: the reservation is of free Liberties and free Customs had in time passed; another is not mentioned.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

§§ `First paragraph: the King shall observe; all Men of the Realm shall observe`

-- gloss (not the text's): "these Customs and Liberties aforesaid, which We have granted to be holden within this our Realm" are
-- the ones the Charter grants; "as much as appertaineth to Us and our Heirs" is the part that falls to the King and his Heirs,
-- which the text does not measure: the asker states both as facts. The text says "we shall observe", so only the King is named.
-- The extract's variant table has "on our part, towards our men," for "as much as appertaineth to Us and our Heirs, we shall observe; and"
-- (fork F-SAVING-VAR2). The text as printed is encoded.
-- src:41 | And all these Customs and Liberties aforesaid, which We have granted to be holden within this our Realm, as much as appertaineth to Us and our Heirs, we shall observe;
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, "we shall observe" (src:41)
GIVEN `it is one of the Customs and Liberties aforesaid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appertaineth to the King and his Heirs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Coverage
`first paragraph: the King shall observe` `it is one of the Customs and Liberties aforesaid` `it appertaineth to the King and his Heirs` MEANS
    IF `it is one of the Customs and Liberties aforesaid` AND `it appertaineth to the King and his Heirs`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, a marginal reading (fork F-SAVING-APPERTAIN): "as much as appertaineth to Us and our Heirs" is a limiter that could be read as the
    -- equivalent of "only", which would make a Liberty that does not appertain to the King one he is not bound to observe. The words are not "only", so silence.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- THE REGULATIVE FORM; traced, not asserted. The text gives no time limit, so no WITHIN: the duty stands until performed.
-- src:41 | we shall observe;
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, "we shall observe" (src:41)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`first paragraph: the King shall observe the Customs and Liberties` MEANS
    PARTY `the King`
    MUST `observe the Customs and Liberties`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "first paragraph: the King did not observe the Customs and Liberties"

-- gloss (not the text's): "as much as in them is" is read as "so far as lies within their power"; the asker states whether it does.
-- "Spiritual" and "Temporal" are both named, so both are covered. "against all Persons" is carried as a phrase, not modelled.
-- The extract's variant table has "shall observe on their part towards their men" for "as much as in them is, shall observe the same
-- against all Persons, in like wise." (fork F-SAVING-VAR3). The text as printed is encoded.
-- src:41 | and all Men of this our Realm, as well Spiritual as Temporal, as much as in them is, shall observe the same against all Persons, in like wise.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, "all Men of this our Realm shall observe" (src:41)
GIVEN `the person is a Man of this our Realm` IS A BOOLEAN
      `observing lies within his power` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Coverage
`first paragraph: a Man of the Realm shall observe` `the person is a Man of this our Realm` `observing lies within his power` MEANS
    IF `the person is a Man of this our Realm` AND `observing lies within his power`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, a marginal reading (fork F-SAVING-INTHEM): "as much as in them is" is a limiter that could be read as the equivalent of "only",
    -- which would make a Man who is not able one who is not bound. The words are not "only", so silence. The judgment is the asker's (and
    -- F-SAVING-VAR3 notes a variant reading).
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- THE REGULATIVE FORM; traced, not asserted. No time limit is stated.
-- src:41 | and all Men of this our Realm, as well Spiritual as Temporal, as much as in them is, shall observe the same against all Persons, in like wise.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, "all Men of this our Realm shall observe" (src:41)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`first paragraph: a Man of the Realm shall observe the Customs and Liberties` MEANS
    PARTY `a Man of the Realm`
    MUST `observe the Customs and Liberties`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `a Man of the Realm` BECAUSE "first paragraph: a Man of the Realm did not observe the Customs and Liberties"

§§ `First paragraph: the subsidy`

-- What the gift is given for, in the text's own words. The text names two things: "this our Gift and Grant of these Liberties", and "other
-- contained in our Charter of Liberties of our Forest". That Charter is not among the sources: its content is not supplied, and the
-- second phrase is carried exactly as the text gives it, without the noun the text leaves out.
-- src:41 | And for this our Gift and Grant of these Liberties, and of other contained in our Charter of Liberties of our Forest,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, what the Fifteenth Part is given for (src:41)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`first paragraph: what the Fifteenth Part is given for` MEANS
    LIST
        "this our Gift and Grant of these Liberties"
        "other contained in our Charter of Liberties of our Forest"

-- gloss (not the text's): "have given" is past: a statement of what the named classes gave "for this our Gift and Grant of these
-- Liberties, and of other contained in our Charter of Liberties of our Forest". It is carried as a stated fact and a ratio, not as a
-- duty to give: the text says nothing about a debt that is still to be paid (fork F-SAVING-GIFT).
-- src:41 | the Archbishops, Bishops, Abbots, Priors, Earls, Barons, Knights, Freeholders, and other our Subjects, have given unto Us the Fifteenth Part of all their Moveables.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, who have given (src:41)
GIVEN capacity IS A Capacity
GIVETH A Coverage
`first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of Moveables was given by` capacity MEANS
    IF      capacity EQUALS `an Archbishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Bishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Abbot`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Prior`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Earl`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Baron`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Knight`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Freeholder`
         OR capacity EQUALS `another Subject`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, unclosed list: the classes are listed, not closed with "only"; another class is not addressed.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- The Fifteenth Part of all their Moveables: one fifteenth of the value of the giver's Moveables, in whatever unit the asker uses.
-- src:41 | have given unto Us the Fifteenth Part of all their Moveables.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, the Fifteenth Part (src:41)
GIVEN `the value of all their Moveables` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of` `the value of all their Moveables` MEANS
    `the value of all their Moveables` DIVIDED BY 15

§§ `First paragraph: the promise the King made on the other part`

-- "unto them" are the classes who have given. The promise: neither We nor our Heirs shall procure or do any thing whereby the
-- Liberties in this Charter contained shall be infringed or broken. The ( ) gap is the extract's; its variant is "for us and our
-- heirs," (fork F-SAVING-VAR4).
-- src:41 | And We have granted unto them on the other part, ( ) that neither We nor our Heirs shall procure or do any thing whereby the Liberties in this Charter contained shall be infringed or broken.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, who the promise is granted unto (src:41)
GIVEN capacity IS A Capacity
GIVETH A Coverage
`first paragraph: the promise is granted unto` capacity MEANS
    `first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of Moveables was given by` capacity

-- The prohibition on the King and on his Heirs, as a decision over the facts: the party, and whether the thing procured or done
-- infringes or breaks the Liberties in this Charter contained. A Man of the Realm is not named by this promise.
-- src:41 | that neither We nor our Heirs shall procure or do any thing whereby the Liberties in this Charter contained shall be infringed or broken.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, the promise (src:41)
GIVEN party IS A Party
      `the thing procured or done infringes or breaks the Liberties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Prohibition
`first paragraph: the King and his Heirs shall not procure or do` party `the thing procured or done infringes or breaks the Liberties` MEANS
    IF      (party EQUALS `the King` OR party EQUALS `an Heir of the King`)
        AND `the thing procured or done infringes or breaks the Liberties`
    THEN `forbidden by the text`
    -- not covered, a marginal reading (fork F-SAVING-WHEREBY): "any thing whereby the Liberties ... shall be infringed or broken" marks what is forbidden
    -- and says nothing of the rest, so a non-infringing act is neither forbidden nor permitted by this sentence. The other reading takes the "whereby"
    -- clause as the whole of what the King and his Heirs promise, so that an act that does not infringe is free. The text does not say that, so silence.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- THE REGULATIVE FORM; traced, not asserted. "neither We nor our Heirs" is two prohibitions, one on each; no WITHIN, so each
-- stands for ever.
-- src:41 | that neither We nor our Heirs shall procure or do any thing whereby the Liberties in this Charter contained shall be infringed or broken.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, the promise (src:41)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`first paragraph: neither the King nor his Heirs shall procure an infringement` MEANS
    (PARTY `the King`
     SHANT `procure or do a thing whereby the Liberties are infringed or broken`
     HENCE FULFILLED
     LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "first paragraph: the King procured or did a thing whereby the Liberties are infringed or broken")
    RAND
    (PARTY `an Heir of the King`
     SHANT `procure or do a thing whereby the Liberties are infringed or broken`
     HENCE FULFILLED
     LEST BREACH BY `an Heir of the King` BECAUSE "first paragraph: an Heir of the King procured or did a thing whereby the Liberties are infringed or broken")

§§ `First paragraph: a thing procured contrary to the premises`

-- gloss (not the text's): "the premises" are what the paragraph has said before; the asker says whether the thing procured is
-- contrary to them. "any person" is any person, including one who is not a Man of the Realm.
-- src:41 | And if any thing be procured by any person contrary to the premises, it shall be had of no force nor effect.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, first paragraph, "of no force nor effect" (src:41)
GIVEN `it was procured by any person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is contrary to the premises` IS A BOOLEAN
-- The sentence is introduced by "if": a thing procured by any person contrary to the premises is of no force nor effect. When that condition
-- fails (a thing not procured by any person, or procured but not contrary to the premises) the text does not make the thing void: the "if" is
-- the text's own word for the condition, so the answer is the distinct no-valued `not made void by ...`, as for the final clause of the second
-- paragraph (fork F-SAVING-VOID). Every input of this rule is addressed by the sentence, so there is no "not covered" branch.
GIVETH A Effect
`first paragraph: a thing procured` `it was procured by any person` `it is contrary to the premises` MEANS
    IF `it was procured by any person` AND `it is contrary to the premises`
    THEN `of no force nor effect`
    -- the "if" fails, so the text does not make it void; this is the text's own condition, not silence.
    ELSE `not made void by the first unnumbered paragraph`

§§ `Second paragraph: ratified, confirmed, made strong, renewed; to be observed`

-- gloss (not the text's): "these Gifts and Grants aforesaid" are the gifts and grants the text above states. This encoding lists the
-- four this text shows and says "not covered" of any other (fork F-SAVING-AFORESAID). "perpetually" binds the King and his Heirs.
-- src:44 | We, Ratifying and approving these Gifts and Grants aforesaid, confirm and make strong all the same for Us and our Heirs perpetually, and by the Tenor of these Presents do renew the same:
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, second paragraph, the confirmation (src:44)
GIVEN gift IS A `Gift or grant`
GIVETH A Confirmation
`second paragraph: what is confirmed, made strong and renewed` gift MEANS
    CONSIDER gift
        -- not covered, silent: only "these Gifts and Grants aforesaid" are ratified, and which they are is F-SAVING-AFORESAID.
        WHEN `a gift or grant this text does not show` THEN `not covered by the second unnumbered paragraph`
        OTHERWISE `confirmed and made strong for the King and his Heirs perpetually, and renewed`

-- "that this Charter and all and singular his Articles for ever shall be stedfastly, firmly, and inviolably observed": a duty in the
-- passive, with no agent. No party is named to observe, so none is invented (fork F-SAVING-NOAGENT): the answer is NOTHING.
-- src:44 | Willing and granting for Us and our Heirs, that this Charter and all and singular his Articles for ever shall be stedfastly, firmly, and inviolably observed;
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, second paragraph, "shall be ... observed" (src:44)
GIVEN `it is this Charter or one of its Articles` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Coverage
`second paragraph: the Charter and its Articles shall be observed for ever` `it is this Charter or one of its Articles` MEANS
    IF `it is this Charter or one of its Articles`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, silent: the sentence is about the Charter and its Articles.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- src:44 | Willing and granting for Us and our Heirs, that this Charter and all and singular his Articles for ever shall be stedfastly, firmly, and inviolably observed;
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, second paragraph, who is to observe (src:44)
GIVETH A MAYBE Party
`second paragraph: the party the text names to observe` MEANS NOTHING

-- gloss (not the text's): "hitherto" is the time of the Letters Patents; the attestation's date is not converted (see NOTES.md).
-- The extract's variant table has "the Charter aforesaid in" for "this Charter and" (fork F-SAVING-VAR5), "even although" for "and if"
-- (F-SAVING-VAR6), and "Not in Original" for the whole closing command (F-SAVING-VAR7). The text as printed is encoded.
-- src:44 | and if any Article in the same Charter contained yet hitherto peradventure hath not been kept We will and by authority royal command from henceforth firmly they be observed.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, second paragraph, the closing command (src:44)
GIVEN `it is an Article of the same Charter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it hath hitherto been kept` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Command
`second paragraph: an Article that hath not been kept` `it is an Article of the same Charter` `it hath hitherto been kept` MEANS
    IF `it is an Article of the same Charter` AND NOT (`it hath hitherto been kept`)
    THEN `commanded to be firmly observed from henceforth`
    -- not commanded: the clause is introduced by "if any Article ... hath not been kept"; for an Article that has been kept it says nothing (a condition, not an "only if").
    ELSE `not commanded by the final clause`

§§ `Third paragraph: the attestation (carried, not a rule)`

-- Inert (reason 1: the date of enactment and the closing words). The date is carried exactly as the text gives it and is not
-- converted to a modern calendar date.
-- src:47 | In Witness whereof We have caused these our Letters Patents to be made. T. Edward our son at Westminster, the twenty-eighth day of March in the twenty-eighth year of our Reign.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), General Saving, third paragraph, the attestation (src:47)
GIVETH A STRING
`third paragraph: the attestation, as the text gives it` MEANS
    "In Witness whereof We have caused these our Letters Patents to be made. T. Edward our son at Westminster, the twenty-eighth day of March in the twenty-eighth year of our Reign."
